# Introduction business process Analysis

In recent years, Business Process Analysis has gained
considerable attention in organisations due to the
necessity of maintaining current practices while also
seeking ways to be competitive and to create value
for stakeholders.

There is no point in making assumptions about
improvements, as these may be unwarranted.
Instead, organisations should critically examine
individual stages of a process to identify process
delays, duplication of work, potential hazards,
risks, bottlenecks, and opportunities for process
improvement.

A visual and objective understanding of how
business processes work, together with a clear
indication of how they might be improved before
making any changes, can justify those changes
and help organisations develop more effective
solutions.

Within the purpose of this portfolio, I propose
to conduct research that explores the practice
of process analysis.

I have studied this topic with research supported
by process-oriented literature to develop a better
understanding of Business Process Analysis and
its importance in organisations.

I examined a range of techniques utilised within
the context of business process analysis and
identified areas where organisations can recognise
the need for improvements and recommend
appropriate solutions for process enhancement.

## 1. Business Process Model and Notation (BPMN) 

Business Process Model and Notation (BPMN) 2.0 is one of the
most widely adopted standards and techniques used in
Business Process Management (BPM).

It provides a standard set of symbols that are used to
create business process diagrams, making them easy to
understand for managers, business analysts, and system
designers.

BPMN diagrams illustrate business processes through a
series of activities, events, gateways, and decision
points, showing the overall flow of a process.

Organisations use BPMN to document and describe their
business processes in a standard format. This makes it
easier to analyse existing processes, identify areas for
improvement, and design more efficient future processes.


## 2. SIPOC Model

The SIPOC model stands for **Suppliers, Inputs, Process,
Outputs, and Customers**. It is a high-level business
process modelling technique that provides an overall view
of a business process.

The SIPOC model is commonly used at the beginning of Lean
and Six Sigma projects to define the scope of a process
before detailed analysis begins.

It helps organisations understand how the major elements
of a process interact by identifying suppliers, required
inputs, the process itself, expected outputs, and the
customers who receive those outputs.

Using a SIPOC model enables organisations to gain a
shared understanding of a process and identify areas
where improvements can be made.

## Process Analysis in Different Business Situations

Process analysis should be conducted when an organisation
wants to improve the efficiency and effectiveness of its
business processes. It should be an ongoing activity,
supported by continuous monitoring so that improvement
opportunities can be identified before problems become
serious.

It should also be performed whenever significant events
occur, such as customer complaints, operational failures,
or unexpected changes in business activities. Analysing
the process at these times helps organisations identify
the root causes of problems and take corrective action.

During strategic planning, process analysis ensures that
existing business processes support the organisation's
goals and long-term objectives. It also helps determine
whether changes are needed to improve future performance.

When organisations experience performance issues such as
high costs, delays, low productivity, or reduced customer
satisfaction, process analysis should be carried out to
identify inefficiencies and recommend suitable solutions.

Process analysis is also essential when implementing new
technologies, during mergers or acquisitions,
organisational restructuring, and when new legal or
regulatory requirements require changes to existing
business processes.
